gm-method (a 'feature X does not work' report where the server pipeline is code-complete: eliminate the server FIRST with a real-service exec_js before touching the client). For 'physics objects can't rotate': a headless exec_js that inits the REAL physics world, adds a dynamic body with angular velocity, steps it, and reads the entity transform over time DECISIVELY proves the server sim + sync writes the evolving quaternion (witnessed: quat evolved smoothly, sync true every step). That one 400ms run eliminates the entire server half (sim, body DOF, sync-writeback, broadcast encode) and forces the search onto the client render path -- far cheaper than fighting a live follow-camera. The real client gap was then found by READING the per-snapshot apply: a rendering fast-path (an instanced/batched ModelPool with a position-only setTarget(x,y,z) + a position-only GPU lerp) silently drops rotation for every entity it routes, while the ordinary scene-graph path applies the quaternion fine. GENERAL: a per-object interpolation/mover API that takes only (x,y,z) is a structural rotation-dropper; when 'X only affects SOME objects', suspect the object is on a DIFFERENT (optimized) render tier whose transform API is narrower than the general path. Also: teleporting a player by mutating client localState.position does NOT move a follow-camera slaved to the reconciled/interpolated mesh -- it snaps back every frame; to witness open-world render state either drive the real input/pointer-lock, render an offscreen RT from a camera you own, or witness the invariant at SOURCE instead of via the live camera.